      "id": 1,
      "text": "Founded by Francisco Pizarro on January 6, 1535, Lima began as the capital of the Spanish Viceroyalty of Peru.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.britannica.com/place/Lima/History"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The historic Puente de los Suspiros in Barranco still draws visitors with its romantic 19th‑century stone arches.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://lima.info/discover-lima-peru-essential-travel-information/"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Lima’s modern growth accelerated after the 1851 completion of the Lima‑Callao railroad, linking the capital to its port.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.britannica.com/place/Lima/History"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "During the War of the Pacific (1881‑83), Chilean forces looted and occupied Lima, burning the National Library in the process.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.britannica.com/place/Lima/History"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Literary revival followed the war, with Ricardo Palma’s “Tradiciones Peruanas” (1872‑1910) reshaping Peru’s cultural narrative.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.britannica.com/place/Lima/History"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Lima’s historic center showcases stunning colonial architecture, blending Spanish baroque with local influences in its streets and plazas.",
